# What the SDIST carries beyond the package itself.
#
# ⭐ WITHOUT THIS FILE THE RELEASED SDIST SHIPS A BROKEN TEST SUITE. setuptools' default sdist
# contents include `tests/test*.py` but NOT `tests/conftest.py`, so the tarball attached to every
# GitHub Release contained the tests and not the fixtures they depend on: two tests failed
# outright, and — the part that actually matters — the autouse fixture that makes "no test in this
# suite reaches the network" TRUE was simply absent from the artifact.
#
# That also quietly weakened the contract in README.md that "tests travel with the module", for
# anyone consuming the sdist rather than the repository. Tests that travel without their
# conftest have not travelled.
include MANIFEST.in
include README.md
include CHANGELOG.md
include pyproject.toml
recursive-include tests *.py

# ⛔ `scripts/` is DELIBERATELY NOT INCLUDED. It briefly was, on the reasoning that a consumer
# auditing the tarball should be able to run the same scan CI runs. That reasoning was thin — the
# guard is a CI tool for THIS repository, not part of the library — and it had a real cost: the
# canonical guard's docstring enumerates the fleet's other repositories by name, so including it
# put that inventory inside every tarball attached to every public GitHub Release, widening the
# distribution of something the tests were edited to stop publishing. Anyone who wants the guard
# can clone the repository, which is where a CI tool belongs.
